# FormulaCode Docker Build Verification Guide

You are fixing a Docker build context for the FormulaCode dataset. The goal of
this workspace is to produce a Docker image in which the upstream repository
**at the base commit** (`{{ sha }}`) builds cleanly, the project package
imports, ASV benchmarks discover, and the upstream pytest suite runs without
import errors. Your work is iterative: run `local_ci.py`, read the real tool
output it captures, fix the underlying problem, run again. Treat `local_ci.py`
as a tool — the local equivalent of upstream CI — that builds the docker image
and runs the upstream test/benchmark suite, then surfaces whatever the
underlying tools (Docker, pip, pytest, asv) actually said.

## Hard requirements

1. You MUST execute `python3 local_ci.py` from the workspace root. This is
   the ONLY accepted way to check whether the build works. A `bash -n` syntax
   check, a manual `pip install`, a manual `docker build`, or any "local
   reproduction" outside this script does NOT count and will be ignored by
   the harness.
2. The ONLY accepted success state is `task/verification_success.json`
   existing on disk when you exit. If that file is missing, the attempt is
   recorded as a failure no matter what edits you made or how confident you
   are in the fix.
3. Other workers may be running concurrently on this host — their processes
   are visible via `ps` but **must be left alone**. Never run `pkill`, `kill`,
   `killall`, or `pgrep` against `local_ci`, `docker`, `asv`, `pytest`,
   `python`, or any process you didn't start yourself. Killing peer processes
   corrupts other tasks.
4. A full `local_ci.py` run kicks off a multi-stage Docker build that
   typically takes **15–40 minutes**, sometimes up to 60. Do not exit while a
   build is still running. Wait for `local_ci.py` to finish and write its
   result file before deciding what to do next. Budget your turns accordingly
   — a single end-to-end run is normal and expected.

## Quick Start

```bash
python3 local_ci.py
```

This runs the full local-CI pipeline (build → tests). On failure it writes
`task/failure.json`. On success it writes `task/verification_success.json`.
Both stages must succeed for the run to be recorded as a success.

